  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nDry weather is expected through Tuesday night as a high pressure \nlingers over the region. Highs in the 80s and low in the 60s will be \nin place for the week. Temperatures look to approach 90 degrees for \nhighs by Sunday and Monday, with increasing humidity throughout the \nweek. The LREF shows a 20-40% chance of apparent temperatures being \ngreater than 95 degrees on Sunday afternoon and 40-60% chance on \nMonday afternoon across the entire forecast area. The CPC has both \nthe 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks with 60-70% chance of above normal \ntemperatures, so the sweltering weather isn't over quite yet for \nthis summer.\n\nA more active pattern will return Wednesday as the upper flow gets \ndisturbed by a 500 mb short wave that will push a cold front into \nthe state. The latest global models (GFS, Euro) are showing an area \nof showers scraping the northern edge of our forecast area overnight \nTuesday. Then, they are showing the main complex of convection \nWednesday afternoon/evening moving north of a McDonough County to \nMcLean County line with additional showery activity south of there \novernight into Thursday. Although, the exact location may\ncontinue to shift as we approach midweek. There is no shortage \nof CAPE Wednesday afternoon, with MUCAPE values (and similarly \nSBCAPE values) around 1500-2500 J/kg and bulk shear values of \n30-50 knots. SRH values look to be around 200-300 in the \nvicinity of the complex. This should sufficiently support \nthunderstorm activity. We could see some severe weather on \nWednesday afternoon, focused north of I-72, closer to I-74. The\nSPC has introduced a marginal risk for severe weather on D3 \n(Wednesday).\n\n\nThe front on Wednesday could possibly stall out over the area, \nbringing additional rounds of showers and thunderstorms into the \nweekend next week. However, latest runs of the global models are \ndepicting more of a dry pattern for later in the week into the \nweekend. We will continue to monitor the shower and thunderstorm \npossibilities beyond Thursday morning as we move forward in the \nweek.",
